Point 2 is reached from point 1 by a change in Easting (D7) and a change in Northing (E7). Bearing is measured clockwise from north, so this formula uses `ATAN2(dN, dE)`. That is `ATAN2(E7,D7)`, because Excel's `ATAN2` takes the x-value first.

`DEGREES` converts the result from radians. `MOD(...,360)` keeps the bearing between 0 and 360°.

{"formula": "=MOD(DEGREES(ATAN2(E7,D7)),360)"}

The headwind should be the headwind component of the wind, matching how the crosswind in X10 is built: X10 uses `ABS(X8*SIN(DEGREES(U7-X7)))`.

So the headwind is the same expression with COS instead of SIN, and without ABS. Leaving out ABS keeps the sign, so a negative value shows a tailwind.

{"formula": "=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))"}

Column M is labelled "Time Delta" and sits beside N, which already has `=ABS(E2-K2)` for the cost difference. The matching guess for time is the absolute difference between Time A (D2) and Time B (J2).

The times are shown as text like "Jan 5 2012 12:15:32.12". If they are stored as text rather than real date-time values, this formula will return `#VALUE!` and the times would need converting first.

{"formula": "=ABS(D2-J2)"}

K6 is the Forward Contract Stop Distance, which is the distance between the entry and the stop. Since the entry order (J6) is the signal price adjusted from TradingView to the IG forward contract, the stop should be adjusted the same way. Adjusted stop = E6-(H6-I6), and stop distance = ABS(J6-(E6-(H6-I6))).

That simplifies to ABS(D6-E6), because the same adjustment applies to both prices. The simpler form gives the same result, so I'll use it.

{"formula": "=ABS(D6-E6)"}

Column D already looks up the refund rate with `AGGREGATE` on the shortage table. B33 can use the same pattern to pull the matching fixed deduction from column C (C3:C11) instead.

For example, a shortage of -250 matches rows 8–11. The smallest fixed deduction there is -170, which belongs to the same row as the 7% rate D33 picks.

{"formula": "=AGGREGATE(15,6,C$3:C$11/(ROUND(A$3:A$11,0)>=A33),1)"}

Table 2 has the same Group, Code and Criterion columns as Table 1, so K4 most likely looks up the matching "Created" timestamp from Table 1. This formula returns the first row in Table 1 where all three values match, and leaves the cell blank if there is no match.

Some combinations appear more than once in Table 1. For example, 123459 / DD / true matches both row 20 and row 23. This formula returns the first match (row 20). If you wanted the earliest or latest date instead, MINIFS or MAXIFS would fit better.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(INDEX($D$4:$D$39,MATCH(1,($A$4:$A$39=H4)*($B$4:$B$39=I4)*($C$4:$C$39=J4),0)),\"\")"}

The "Percentage by event" header in D12 suggests you want the average satisfaction rate across the five events, so I predict an average of row 3 for "Very satisfied".

One risk: cells like B3 show "9, 64.3%". If those cells hold that literal text rather than separate numbers, AVERAGE will ignore them or return #DIV/0!. In that case the formula would need to extract the percentage from each cell. If the percentages work correctly, the five values average to about 69.2%.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(B3:F3)"}

U1 is headed "Rank Mertics", so U2 should rank the row. Column S2 holds the top value as text like "3 (60%)", so the most useful metric is the leading count from that text.

The ranking itself is unclear: there's only one data row (row 2), so there's nothing to rank against. The safest guess is to return the numeric count from S2, the number before the first space.

{"formula": "=--LEFT(S2,FIND(\" \",S2)-1)"}

Each row above (H9–H12) counts how often its label in column G appears in the Description column (P). The most likely next step is to continue that pattern for "Other" in G13.

One caveat: "Other" doesn't currently appear anywhere in column P, so this formula will return 0 unless "Other" entries are added. If you meant "everything not already counted," a different formula would be needed.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(P:P,G13)"}

Row 39 is the "Total" row, and the neighboring columns D, F and G all use `=SUM(col8:col38)`. Following that pattern, E39 should be `=SUM(E8:E38)`.

Note that column E only contains "<1" text entries, so this SUM will return 0. It still matches the pattern the user is most likely following.

{"formula": "=SUM(E8:E38)"}

B13 is the "Units" cell for the Community column. It probably follows the pattern of C13 next to it, which uses `=IF(LH_OI="Yes",COUNTIF(LH_Sites,">0"),"")` for Leased Homes. The Community cells below it (like B14) are gated on `Community_OI="Yes"`. So the most likely formula counts the community rent-roll units when the community is included.

Using `COUNTA` on `Rent_Roll_Unit_Type` is my best guess. The workbook may intend a different count, such as only occupied sites.

{"formula": "=IF(Community_OI=\"Yes\",COUNTA(Rent_Roll_Unit_Type),\"\")"}

M3 is the column-A cell in the Statistics block. Its neighbours N3:Q3 all use the same pattern, shifting only the column, so M3 follows it with column A:

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(OFFSET(A$3,$J$2-3,0,$J$3-$J$2+1,1),$L3)"}

This copies the existing count formula. It does not yet stop values that depend on rows below End row from being counted, which is the problem described in H18:H23.

E2 sits where segment "Bakers" (D2) meets the "0 - 50" band (E1), so the formula should count the orders for that segment whose untaxed amount falls in that band.

Using COUNTIFS against segments in column A and amounts in column B, the "0 - 50" band is amounts of 50 or less. Values above 0 are included, so the lower bound is `>=0`.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($A$2:$A$337,$D2,$B$2:$B$337,\">=0\",$B$2:$B$337,\"<=50\")"}

Row 17 labels the columns: H is "Yes", I is "Yes (xS)", J is "NE", K is "No", L is "Injured" and M is "Out". Row 19 is the L2 row, so J19 should count players marked "NE" at level 2.

"NE" carries no level in the Decision column, so the level has to come from the Player Code in column C. Level-2 codes end in "2" (O2 or S2), which the "*2" wildcard matches. The ranges follow the same `$D5:$D93` style used in H19.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($C5:$C93,\"*2\",$D5:$D93,\"NE\")"}

G3 is the interest for months 1–36, so it should use CUMIPMT, mirroring the CUMPRINC call in H3: `=CUMIPMT(E3/12,D5,F3,1,36,0)`.

The rate is E3/12. The number of periods is D5, which is 180 months, the total loan term. F3 is the loan amount.

{"formula": "=CUMIPMT(E3/12,D5,F3,1,36,0)"}

The "Workings" section rebuilds the main formula step by step. F9 pulls out the month name with `MID(F1,4,10)`. F10, "Month start date", matches the `d` variable in the main formula: `DATE(YEAR($A2),MONTH(1&MID(F$1,4,10)),1)`.

Since F9 already holds the month name, F10 can reference it directly, using row 2's start date for the year.

{"formula": "=DATE(YEAR($A2),MONTH(1&F9),1)"}

The "Date Value" column most likely needs the date pulled out of the "First Join" entry in C2. That entry looks like text in the form `11/20/23, 2:02:13 PM`. This formula takes everything before the comma and converts it to a date with `DATEVALUE`.

If C2 is actually a real date-time value rather than text, this formula would return an error. In that case `=INT(C2)` would be the right choice instead.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(LEFT(C2,FIND(\",\",C2)-1))"}

Column G is "Days from lease start to today", and the reference date sits in J1 (labelled "Date Used In Duration Calculation" in G1). So G3 should be that date minus the commencement date in D3.

J1 needs absolute anchoring so the formula still points to it when filled down the column.

{"formula": "=$J$1-D3"}

Column P is labelled DELTA. For a call option with dividend yield O and time M, delta is N(d1) multiplied by the dividend discount factor, where d1 is already computed in column N.

Following the style of the gamma formula in column Q, that gives:

{"formula": "=EXP(-1*M2*O2)*NORMSDIST(N2)"}

The row is labelled "Discount Rate", and the only rate in the inputs is the YTM in C10, so I14 most likely just pulls that value in.

I've made the reference absolute (`$C$10`) so it stays fixed when the formula is copied across to J14 and K14. Without that, the reference would shift to D10 and E10, which are empty. That is the same problem row 16 already has: J16 and K16 point at D10 and E10 instead of C10.

{"formula": "=$C$10"}

G3 sits under the "GCD" header, and H3 and I3 divide C3 and D3 by G3 to build the ratio. So G3 most likely holds the greatest common divisor of C3 and D3.

Note that `GCD` truncates decimals to integers. With values like 1826.64 and 566.33, the ratio in H3 and I3 may come out as non-whole numbers.

{"formula": "=GCD(C3,D3)"}

I7 sits under the "CD %" header and should return a cash-discount rate based on the number of days in H7. The table in K2:L5 is a days-to-rate slab: 0.1 maps to 10%, 35.01 to 8%, 50.01 to 6%, and 65.1 to 0%.

Because the K column is sorted ascending, an approximate-match lookup returns the rate for the highest threshold at or below H7. The rate should only apply to payment rows, where F7 is greater than 0, so other rows return an empty string.

{"formula": "=IF(F7>0,LOOKUP(H7,$K$2:$K$5,$L$2:$L$5),\"\")"}

I read E51 ("Nominal") as the nominal pipe size for the flow in C51: the smallest diameter whose velocity in that flow row is at or below the limit in D48 (5 FPS). This is a best guess from the layout.

The formula:
- uses the row number in D51 to get that flow's row of the table (`D51-3`, because the data starts at row 4);
- considers only the "Velocity FPS" columns and skips blank cells;
- returns the diameter from row 2 for the first column that meets the limit.

{"formula": "=INDEX($B$2:$O$2,MATCH(TRUE,INDEX((INDEX($B$4:$O$46,D51-3,0)<=$D$48)*(INDEX($B$4:$O$46,D51-3,0)>0)*($B$3:$O$3=\"Velocity FPS\")>0,0),0))"}

Column E holds the interest for each month, so E10 should be the balance in B10 multiplied by the monthly rate. Using the same 0.73109% rate as the PMT formulas in column C gives `=B10*0.73109%`.

This checks out: the earlier rows imply 12063.0281 / 1650000 = 0.0073109.

{"formula": "=B10*0.73109%"}

M7 sits under "Longest Warnings", so it should mirror I7 (shortest warnings) but use LARGE instead of SMALL, with the same date-filter condition and rank from L7:

{"formula": "=LARGE(IF(($B$7:$B$29>=$C$3)+($B$7:$B$29<=$E$3),$C$7:$C$29),L7)"}

The `+` in the date condition is copied from I7, but it acts as OR. That means every row passes the filter rather than only dates between C3 and E3. If you want the date range applied properly, swap `+` for `*` in both this formula and I7.

E3 most likely wants the average speed for the KP in D3, taken from the rows where column B matches it. AVERAGEIF does that:

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF($B$3:$B$42,D3,$A$3:$A$42)"}

The range stops at row 42 because that is as far as the visible data goes; if column A/B continues further down, the range should be extended to the last row.

B7 is labelled "Rate Card", and F7:H7 all pull their retention rate from it. E9 hard-codes 8.5% where F9:H9 use B7, which suggests B7 should carry the same 8.5% rate that sits in E7.

This is a best guess rather than a clear-cut prediction. The 0 to 2,000,000 thresholds in Q1:Q43 might be meant for a tiered rate lookup on turnover. No matching column of rates is visible, though, so I went with the simpler reading.

{"formula": "=E7"}

Cell J6 is labelled "Low_Max peak discharge in continuous pulse_15 Days", and the formulas above it use `MAXIFS` over rows 19–33. I followed that pattern: the formula returns the largest flow (column B) among low-flow rows (C=1) whose helper count (column F) equals the longest continuous run length in J5.

One limitation: this only matches the last day of the longest run, where the counter reaches its maximum. It gives the flow on that day, not necessarily the peak across the whole run. A true peak-within-run would need a more complex formula.

{"formula": "=MAXIFS($B$19:$B$33,$C$19:$C$33,1,$F$19:$F$33,J5)"}

U2 sits under the "Highest" column. These look like weekly rankings, where 1 is the best, so "Highest" most likely means the best ranking, which is the smallest number. That points to MIN, although MAX is also a plausible reading.

The Average column next to it uses `=AVERAGE(B2:S2)`, so I'll follow the same range. I'm going with MIN on that reading of the rankings.

{"formula": "=MIN(B2:S2)"}

O6 is the "FY" total column for the product rows. O7 and O8 already show yearly totals, but O6 is empty. That suggests O6 holds a single formula that spills totals down O6:O8.

This one mirrors the MMULT pattern in C9, which sums columns, but runs it across rows instead. The formula multiplies the C6:N8 block by a column of 1s, giving one row-sum per product. That would make Pear 26, the second product 34 and Apple 16, which adds up to the 76 in O9.

{"formula": "=MMULT(--C6:N8,SEQUENCE(COLUMNS(C6:N8),1,1,0))"}

K2 is headed "Combined Formula", so K3 most likely merges the helper columns into one formula. Column H converts the start time to a fraction of a day, column I does the same for the end time, and J3 is `=MOD(I3-H3,1)+IF(H3=I3,1,0)`.

The formula below substitutes the H3 and I3 expressions into J3's MOD calculation. For the "same start and end time" check, it uses `B3&C3=D3&E3`, the same check column F uses. It counts as a full day when the start and end times match.

{"formula": "=MOD(IF(OR(AND(E3=\"AM\",D3<>12),AND(E3=\"PM\",D3=12)),D3/24,(D3+12)/24)-IF(OR(AND(C3=\"AM\",B3<>12),AND(C3=\"PM\",B3=12)),B3/24,(B3+12)/24),1)+IF(B3&C3=D3&E3,1,0)"}

H8 is the "Time" column, sitting between Start Time (F8) and End Time (G8). I8 multiplies H8 by 24 to get hours, so H8 must hold the elapsed time as a fraction of a day.

The sheet also has night shifts where the start time is later than the end time (it compares F8>G8 elsewhere). Adding MOD(...,1) handles shifts that cross midnight, since a plain G8-F8 would go negative there.

{"formula": "=MOD(G8-F8,1)"}

D18 holds the period length used by the YTD column, so it should be the number of months from the start of the financial year (B4, July 2022) to the YTD end date (D20). With D20 = December 2022, that gives 6 months (July to December), which makes the D21:D28 formulas sum the year to date.

The formula counts the months between B4 and D20 and adds 1 so both the first and last months are included.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF($B$4,D20,\"m\")+1"}

The 9-month block mirrors the 12-month formulas. In F21 the 12-month values are swapped out: 65 days becomes 54, and the divisor 3 becomes 2.5.

Applying the same swaps to E10's formula gives E21. I've kept E10's MROUND to the nearest 0.25 day.

{"formula": "=MROUND(ROUND(B22*54*B23,2)/2.5,0.25)"}

The header says "TAT excluding weekends", so the formula should count working days between the order date (B2) and the completion date (D2). NETWORKDAYS does that.

The vacation dates in column F are free text (for example "December 12 to December 22"), so they can't be passed in as holidays. I've left them out.

{"formula": "=NETWORKDAYS(B2,D2)"}

Cell J4 sits next to the inputs Year (J1), Month (J2) and Percentile (J3 = 95). Column E keeps the daily values for the chosen year and month that are at least 1, and blanks out the rest. So J4 most likely calculates the 95th percentile of column E.

PERCENTILE ignores the blank-text results, and the percentile needs to be a fraction, so J3 is divided by 100. The data appears to run beyond row 249 (it covers 1980–2009), so the formula uses the whole column E.

{"formula": "=PERCENTILE(E:E,J3/100)"}

B9 is labeled Bond Price, and every input needed for a PRICE calculation is on the sheet: settlement, maturity, rate, yield, redemption, frequency and day-count basis. PRICE returns the price per 100 of face value. To express it in the same units as the 1000 redemption value, divide B5 by 100 and multiply the result by that.

{"formula": "=PRICE(B1,B2,B3,B4,B5/10,B6,B7)*10"}

Wait — PRICE's redemption argument also has to be per 100, so the formula should be built consistently:

{"formula": "=PRICE(B1,B2,B3,B4,100,B6,B7)*B5/100"}

The Period number is found by looking up the week number from J4 in the week column (B2:B54) and returning the matching period from column A.

The current week matches by week number. The 13 periods are each 4 weeks long, so another option is `=ROUNDUP(J4/4,0)`. That would give 14 for week 53, which doesn't match column A (week 53 belongs to period 13). The lookup avoids that problem.

{"formula": "=INDEX($A$2:$A$54,MATCH(J4,$B$2:$B$54,0))"}

Under "balloon defn 1", the loan's final balance equals the balloon amount in B23. The regular payment is the PMT that pays the loan down from B15 to B23 over B22 months. It is rounded up to the cent, the same way as the Loan 1 payment in B9.

The balloon is a balance still owed, so it takes the same sign as the loan amount: both are entered as negatives (`-B15` and `-B23`). That keeps the payment positive.

{"formula": "=ROUNDUP(PMT(B16/12,B22,-B15,-B23),2)"}

The printer in row 22 uses straight-line depreciation, and its 10-year useful life ended in 2019, so it is already fully depreciated.

The formula follows the pattern in M19, which is `=SLN(E19,F19,G19)*I19`. It caps the years at the useful life with `MIN(I22,G22)`, so accumulated depreciation stops at cost minus salvage (6,700). This matches N22 being 0 for this asset.

{"formula": "=SLN(E22,F22,G22)*MIN(I22,G22)"}

Cell E3 is labelled "PayOff Balance", so I read it as the remaining balance after the last month that has an actual payment entered. `COUNTIF(G7:G82,">0")` counts how many months have a payment in column G. `INDEX` then returns the Ending Balance (column E) for that month.

This assumes payments are entered in consecutive months starting from row 7. If a month is skipped, the count will point to the wrong row.

{"formula": "=INDEX(E7:E82,COUNTIF(G7:G82,\">0\"))"}

P3 should hold the quantity of the fruit in O3 ("Melons") between the dates in P1 (2-10-2023) and Q1 (8-10-2023). The country filter sits in T3:V6, but it's unclear which cell holds the selected country, so I've left the country out.

The formula sums the date columns D:M across rows 2–29 where the fruit in column C matches O3 and the column-header date in row 1 falls within P1 to Q1. References are anchored so the formula can be copied down to P4:P6.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(($C$2:$C$29=O3)*($D$1:$M$1>=$P$1)*($D$1:$M$1<=$Q$1)*$D$2:$M$29)"}

The pattern down the diagonal is a TRANSPOSE of the row above, starting one column to the right. B24 uses `=TRANSPOSE(C23:K23)`, and I31 uses `=TRANSPOSE(J30:K30)`. Following that step, J32 should transpose K31 alone, which gives `=TRANSPOSE(K31:K31)`.

The simpler `=K31` would return the same value, but I kept the TRANSPOSE form to match the rest of the matrix.

{"formula": "=TRANSPOSE(K31:K31)"}

H9 sits in the "n" column of the range table for variable "a". F9 holds the lower bound and G9 the upper bound of that range. So H9 should count how many values in column B fall within the range.

The bounds chain: F9 is the minimum, G9 = F9+1, F10 = G9, and G11 is the maximum. Because each "to" value becomes the next "from" value, the bins need a half-open interval: include values ≥ the "from" and < the "to". The last bin can't follow that rule, since it ends at the maximum and would otherwise miss the maximum value. That edge case can be handled later, so I'll use the half-open count here.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($B$4:$B$63,\">=\"&F9,$B$4:$B$63,\"<\"&G9)"}

I3 is the "Work Load" header, so I5 probably holds the total load for the Snatch block in F5:H5. I'm assuming that load is sets × reps × weight for each column, summed. The sets are text like "4 x 3" in F5:H5, and the weights are in F6:H6.

`LEFT` picks out the sets and `RIGHT` picks out the reps. This only works because every value in row 5 is a single digit. It would break on rows with two-digit reps such as "2 x 10".

{"formula": "=LEFT(F5,1)*RIGHT(F5,1)*F6+LEFT(G5,1)*RIGHT(G5,1)*G6+LEFT(H5,1)*RIGHT(H5,1)*H6"}

D2 is empty and the prefix is only `=`, so this is an inference. Column C seems to return True when the two lists share at least one size. The comparison looks like it works on whole sizes, not partial text: row 42 (S-L vs XXL-XL) is False even though "XL" contains "L". This formula splits both lists on "-" and checks whether any size in A2 also appears in B2.

One mismatch: row 16 shows True in C, but this formula would return False there, because B16 holds a name rather than sizes.

{"formula": "=OR(ISNUMBER(MATCH(TEXTSPLIT(A2,\"-\"),TEXTSPLIT(B2,\"-\"),0)))"}

Cell N15 says these are 4 of the 8 array formulas tried, and N19, O19 and P19 already hold three of them. So Q19 is most likely the fourth attempt at the same task: listing the non-blank entries from E5:E16 in order.

This guess uses a common variant of that approach. It finds the next non-blank row with `SMALL(IF(...))`, uses a row counter that grows as the formula is copied down, and returns a blank once the list runs out (via `IFERROR`). Since the column and approach are inferred from the neighbouring formulas, this is a best guess rather than a certain match.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(INDEX($E$5:$E$16,SMALL(IF($E$5:$E$16<>\"\",ROW($E$5:$E$16)-ROW($E$5)+1),ROWS(Q$19:Q19))),\"\")"}

Row 2 has a store and a start-of-month date, and the headers C1 to U1 run 1 through 19. That looks like a cohort-style layout where each column is a number of months after the date in column B. My best guess is that C2 should return the date that many months later. This is an inference, since the grid is empty and the intent isn't certain.

`$B2` keeps the date column fixed, and `C$1` keeps the header row fixed, so the formula can be filled across and down.

{"formula": "=EDATE($B2,C$1)"}

Your note says the yellow cells should leave out "XXX" and show each repeated value only once. This formula lists each distinct value from A1:A25 once, skipping "XXX". It needs a version of Excel with dynamic arrays (Excel 365 or 2021).

One assumption: the `[Person_1]` entries in column A look like a privacy placeholder. I've taken them to be the "XXX" values. If the actual text in those cells is different, change `"XXX"` in the formula to match.

{"formula": "=UNIQUE(FILTER(A1:A25,A1:A25<>\"XXX\"))"}

The "Use" block (Q3:V3) has the same crop headers as the fields block (E3:J3). The list in L4:M14 maps each product to the crops it can be used on.

So Q4 should return the customer's field count for that crop (E4) only when the product in P4 is approved for the crop in Q3. Otherwise it returns 0. The Percent block below (Q15 onward) then divides each value by the row total. The column-absolute `$P4` and row-absolute `Q$3` let the formula be filled across and down.

{"formula": "=IF(COUNTIFS($L$4:$L$14,$P4,$M$4:$M$14,Q$3)>0,E4,0)"}

G5 sits in the "Events Open" row, under the month in G3. It should follow the same pattern as G4 (count of rows dated in that month) and add a condition that Status is "Open".

G7 already uses full-column ranges ($A:$A) with a Status = "Open" condition, so I'm following that style. COUNTIFS ignores case, so "open" in B12 and B13 is also counted.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($A:$A,\">=\"&G$3,$A:$A,\"<=\"&EOMONTH(G$3,0),$B:$B,\"Open\")"}

The formula below counts low-flow pulses (Flow Cat = 1) in the 15 days before the date in H2. A pulse is counted where a new run starts (helper F = 1). It is also counted if a run is already going on the first day of the window.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT((E$2:E$67>=H2-15)*(E$2:E$67<H2)*(C$2:C$67=1)*(((F$2:F$67=1)+(E$2:E$67=H2-15))>0))"}

On this data it returns 2, matching the check value in K2. One pulse runs over the start of the window (Nov 18), and a new one starts on Nov 23.

E7 is the "Last Year" row, so it should mirror E6 but for the end date. E6 takes the start year's budget and multiplies it by the fraction of that year remaining after the start date.

For the last year, the formula takes the end year's budget and multiplies it by the fraction of that year elapsed before the end date. The elapsed days run from January 1 of the end year to C3, so the formula is INDEX(H:H,MATCH(YEAR(C3),G:G,0))*(DATEDIF(DATE(YEAR(C3),1,1),C3,"d")/365).

{"formula": "=INDEX(H:H,MATCH(YEAR(C3),G:G,0))*((DATEDIF(DATE(YEAR(C3),1,1),C3,\"d\"))/365)"}

F3 should return the easting of the old point closest to the new point. The formula below finds the smallest straight-line distance from B3/C3 to each old point in L3:M21, then returns that point's easting. E3 already uses F3 to look up the matching gully number.

In older Excel without dynamic arrays, enter it with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=INDEX($L$3:$L$21,MATCH(MIN(($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2),($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2,0))"}

F3 should return the Easting from the old data (column L) of the point nearest to the new point in B3/C3. E3 and G3 already look up `$F3` in column L, so they will then pull the matching GULLY_NO and Northing.

The formula measures the squared distance from (B3, C3) to every old point. It takes the smallest one and returns the Easting at that position. It assumes the old data is in rows 3–21; widen the ranges if there is more.

{"formula": "=INDEX($L$3:$L$21,MATCH(MIN(($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2),($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2,0))"}

J2 should hold the last row where the item in H2 appears in column A. I2 already gives the first row. The items are grouped together in column A, so the last row is the first row plus the number of matches, minus 1.

For example, "8000 2BKA GRW" appears in rows 2–5: I2 = 2, the count is 4, so J2 = 2 + 4 − 1 = 5. K2 then has a valid row range to pull the subs from column B.

{"formula": "=I2+COUNTIF(A:A,$H2)-1"}

Z12 sits on the row labelled "Total Pips Won/Avg". Y12 already holds the total pips won: `=SUMIF(T6:T203,">0")`.

Z12 should therefore be the average pips per winning trade. That is the total in Y12 divided by the number of winning trades, which Z8 holds (`=COUNTIF(U6:U203,">0")`). This matches how Z7 also divides a total by a count.

{"formula": "=Y12/Z8"}

M10 sits under "QC % Per Tech", and its neighbours are "Jobs taken to QC" (K10) and "Total QC Completed" (L10). The likely meaning is the share of jobs taken to QC that were completed: L10/K10.

Column J already uses a guard against zeros, `=IF(AND(G10<>0,H10<>0),H10/G10,0)`, so I've followed the same pattern here. Row 13 has K and L both at 0, so the guard is needed to avoid a divide-by-zero error.

{"formula": "=IF(AND(K10<>0,L10<>0),L10/K10,0)"}

Cell C31 is the "# of closings" for January under the 2009 block. This formula counts posted dates in that month and year. It checks all four "Posted" columns: G, L, Q and V.

It reuses the same `SUMPRODUCT` conditions as the "Total Bonus" formula in D31. The difference is that it drops the bonus-amount factor, so it counts matching rows instead of summing bonuses.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(--($G$2:$G$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($G$2:$G$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($G$2:$G$27)=A$29))+SUMPRODUCT(--($L$2:$L$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($L$2:$L$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($L$2:$L$27)=A$29))+SUMPRODUCT(--($Q$2:$Q$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($Q$2:$Q$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($Q$2:$Q$27)=A$29))+SUMPRODUCT(--($V$2:$V$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($V$2:$V$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($V$2:$V$27)=A$29))"}

The header in AK4 is "Sundays", so AK5 most likely calculates the total hours worked on Sundays. This follows the same pattern as AJ5, which sits under "Night shift" in AJ4.

The formula reuses the hour calculation from AK7, including June 2022, where `WEEKDAY(...,11)=7` picks out Sundays. I removed AK7's extra night-shift condition so that all Sunday shifts are counted.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(IFERROR(IF(VALUE(LEFT(C4:AG4,2))<VALUE(RIGHT(C4:AG4,2)),VALUE(RIGHT(C4:AG4,2))-VALUE(LEFT(C4:AG4,2)),24+VALUE(RIGHT(C4:AG4,2))-VALUE(LEFT(C4:AG4,2))),0)*(IFERROR(WEEKDAY(DATE(2022,6,C3:AG3),11),0)=7))"}

The overs total for Baxter likely follows the same pattern as the other summary cells in row 39. C39 sums the column one to its left (`=SUM(B2:B37)`), so B39 would sum Baxter's overs column A over the same rows.

{"formula": "=SUM(A2:A37)"}

Cricket overs are stored as "overs.balls" (e.g. 4.1 is 4 overs and 1 ball), so a plain SUM can be off once the ball counts add up to 6 or more.

Row 36 holds today's date and negative values, which look like current fund values entered as outflows. That fits an XIRR calculation per fund column, so B37 is likely the XIRR for FUND 03. The `$` on the dates lets the formula copy across to the other fund columns.

One caveat: rows 33–35 are empty, and so are many value cells in each column. XIRR may return an error depending on how your Excel version handles those blanks.

{"formula": "=XIRR(B3:B36,$A$3:$A$36)"}

For N20 (the DB row), I matched N19's `=SLN(E19,F19,G19)` and used the DB function. DB needs one more argument than SLN: the period. I used Years To Date (`I20`) for that.

This only works for DB rows. It doesn't switch on the method in column L, and it doesn't handle land, disposals or assets past their useful life.

{"formula": "=DB(E20,F20,G20,I20)"}

Row 9 is the "less-precise trend", built from the quadratic coefficients in M9:O9 (x², x¹, x⁰). The x values are the event numbers in row 2.

So B9 evaluates the polynomial at B2. The coefficient cells are absolute so the formula can be filled across to K9.

{"formula": "=$M$9*B2^2+$N$9*B2+$O$9"}
